---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import phasePortraits from '../figures/phase-portraits.svg?raw';

const fukami = 'https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-ecolsys-110411-160340';
const goldford = 'https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aat1168';
const estrela = 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cels.2021.09.011';
const scott = 'https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1192588';
const vannette = 'https://doi.org/10.1111/ele.12204';
---

<Base title="Sebastian Correa-Gallego">
  <header class="hero">
    <img
      class="hero__img"
      src="/assets/field.webp"
      alt="Sebastian Correa-Gallego taking samples by headlamp in the Organal San Antonio, a volcaniclastic cave in Támesis, Colombia."
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
    <div class="hero__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__content">
        <h1 class="hero__name">Sebastian<br />Correa-Gallego</h1>
        <p class="hero__degree">B.Sc. in Biology, Universidad EAFIT</p>
        <p class="hero__phrase">
          On how microbial communities assemble, and whether the state they reach can be
          predicted from the organisms that build it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <a class="hero__cue" href="#research" aria-label="Continue to research"><span aria-hidden="true"></span></a>
  </header>

  <section class="focus" id="research">
    <div class="wrap focus__inner">
      <h2 class="focus__lead reveal">
        When does community assembly follow rules, and when does it depend on history?
      </h2>

      <div class="focus__grid">
        <div class="focus__text reveal">
          <p>
            The question is most tractable in microbial community assembly, where one pool of
            organisms may converge on a single reproducible state or settle into any of several,
            depending on which arrives first
            (<a href={fukami} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Fukami 2015</a>). Both outcomes are
            documented. Communities assembled from different natural inocula converge at coarse
            taxonomic levels while diverging at the level of species
            (<a href={goldford} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Goldford et al. 2018</a>), and a
            single pool can reach distinct stable compositions that share a common function
            (<a href={estrela} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Estrela et al. 2022</a>). What is
            missing is a rule that predicts which regime holds from properties of the organisms
            themselves.
          </p>
          <p>
            I ask whether such a rule can be built from how a cell divides its finite proteome
            between growing quickly and growing efficiently, a partition the bacterial growth laws
            made measurable
            (<a href={scott} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Scott et al. 2010</a>). Species traits
            already predict the strength of priority effects better than phylogenetic relatedness
            does, once they are decomposed into what a species requires and how it alters its
            environment
            (<a href={vannette} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Vannette and Fukami 2014</a>).
            Proteome allocation may sit below that decomposition, since it can be measured in an
            organism grown alone, before any competition takes place. The hypothesis is that the
            spread of allocation strategies across a species pool predicts whether assembly
            converges or arrival order decides the outcome.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="focus__figure reveal">
          <Fragment set:html={phasePortraits} />
          <figcaption class="fig__caption">
            Trajectories of a two-species competition model, each starting from a different
            initial composition. Arrows show the direction of flow. On the left, weak
            interspecific competition and a single stable state reached from every start. On the
            right, mutual competition strong enough to make each species able to exclude the
            other, so two stable states sit on either side of a separatrix through an unstable
            saddle. Which one the community reaches depends on where it starts. The axes are the
            abundances of two species, and real communities have many more.
            <span class="fig__source">
              Generated from the Lotka-Volterra competition equations. Source in
              scripts/phase_portraits.py.
            </span>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
